#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204722} :activity/id #uuid "8b70251a-b334-5a59-acbb-a546a9d6c32e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048256 :topic/id #uuid "8cb314f5-21fe-5471-a157-6d01a0435299" :topic/math-academy-id 4927} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205223 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to disprove conditional statements. \n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a conditional statement of the following form:\n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)\n$$\n\nTo disprove this statement, we must show its negation is true. \n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀x\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃x\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove the implication $P\\Rightarrow Q,$ it's sufficient to find one value of the variable such that $P$ and $\\lnot Q$ are both true. This value of $x$ is a *counterexample* that disproves the original implication.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "30ca228b-fb65-5f29-b595-1982fc663520" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8362 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0f8e0e69-6710-5e49-82a0-5871fdb9f048" :step/math-academy-id 162530 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205279 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to disprove the following statement:\n\n*For any $a \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $2 \\mid a,$ then $4 \\mid a$*\n\nBy denoting the predicates $P$ and $Q$ as\n\n- $P(a){:} \\quad 2 \\mid a$\n\n- $Q(a){:} \\quad 4 \\mid a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\: P \\Rightarrow Q.\n$$\n\nWe can disprove this statement by finding a counterexample. In other words, we need to find a value of $a$ such that its negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(2∣a)\\,∧\\,(4∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that* \n\n$$\n2 \\mid a  \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=2$ suffices. \n\n*Let's choose $a = 2.$ Then, we have that*\n\n$$\n2 \\mid 2\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid 2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n*So, $a=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's write down the full argument." :tutorial/id #uuid "e09d3ca0-da11-51c7-8834-d9e55a13ac5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9362 :tutorial/title "Disproving a Divisibility Statement"} :step/id #uuid "b4ace00c-cfe4-51a1-9841-468562e1e1f1" :step/math-academy-id 162531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205277 :tutorial/content "Disprove the following statement:\n\n*For any $a \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ if $2 \\mid a,$ then $4 \\mid a.$*\n\nCounterexample:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that* \n\n$$\n2 \\mid a  \n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid a.\n$$\n\n*Let's choose $a = 2.$ Then, we have that*\n\n$$\n2 \\mid 2\n\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n4 \\not\\mid 2.\n$$\n\n*So, $a=2$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "b019cf05-ce80-5440-9fde-c04187ec70b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9360 :tutorial/title "Stating the Full Counterexample"} :step/id #uuid "3fcc654f-a487-5efc-84ef-833ecdb6b419" :step/math-academy-id 162532 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203933 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203673 :question/id #uuid "6374d4a4-dbe5-5b22-b74b-63099326f833" :question/math-academy-id "e-13355"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca200dca-337f-5c83-b109-4c2f30d0acdc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050369}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419393}] :knowledge-point/title "Stating Counterexample Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "a6c4ce5d-4a27-54a9-b69e-046267002d98" :step/math-academy-id 162533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203934 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203671 :question/id #uuid "5efb9740-c4ea-5dab-b927-336adacf5d60" :question/math-academy-id "e-13340"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0394e4c4-97f7-556c-a7c1-ddf4cde28237" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048604}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419394}] :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Divisibility Statements"} :step/id #uuid "feeb1738-862f-5758-bdc6-229269e60bbf" :step/math-academy-id 162536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204727}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203674 :question/id #uuid "ead69480-6713-5c2f-ad87-c2caa35de588" :question/math-academy-id "e-13356"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfa972be-3517-5432-8d4c-c787e2a63633" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050689}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204214} {:db/id #edb/ref 175